<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Wix xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/wix/2006/wi">
  <!--
    PDF default-handler registration.

    !! DO NOT USE {{handlebars}} PLACEHOLDERS IN THIS FILE !!

    main.wxs is a Tauri template and IS rendered through handlebars, but
    fragmentPaths files are NOT. tauri-bundler 2.9.4
    (src/bundle/windows/msi/mod.rs, the `for input_path in input_paths` loop)
    renders each fragment into a throwaway string used only to sniff which
    WixXxxExtension.dll to load, then hands candle.exe the ORIGINAL on-disk
    path. Whatever is literally in this file is what candle compiles.

    This bit us: the previous revision wrote Value="{{bundle_id}}.pdf" here and
    every install put the literal 15-character string "{{bundle_id}}.pdf" into
    HKCU\Software\Classes\.pdf — a ProgId that does not exist. Verified on a
    real installed machine. Hence the hardcoded identifiers below; they must be
    kept in sync by hand with desktop/tauri.conf.json:

      identifier   asia.lichang.pdf-editor   -> ProgId "asia.lichang.pdf-editor.pdf"
      publisher    Lichang                   -> registry vendor key
      productName  PDF Editor

    Windows reality check: writing the .pdf ProgId does NOT take the default
    away from whatever the user already uses. Since Windows 8,
    HKCU\...\Explorer\FileExts\.pdf\UserChoice wins, and it is hash-protected —
    only the user, through the OS UI, can change it. Anything that forges that
    hash is malware behaviour and is not done here. So we do the two things an
    installer legitimately can:

      1. Register Application Capabilities + RegisteredApplications, which is
         what makes the app show up as a candidate in Settings > Default apps
         (the same mechanism Chrome and Firefox use).
      2. Deep-link the user straight to our page in Settings after install
         (see OpenDefaultAppsSettings in main.wxs) so it is one click, not a
         scavenger hunt.

    The .pdf ProgId write still has a purpose: on a profile that has no
    UserChoice for .pdf yet, it is the effective default.
  -->
  <Fragment>
    <DirectoryRef Id="TARGETDIR">
      <!--
        Unconditional: "this app can open PDFs" is a capability statement, not a
        takeover. It only makes the app selectable in Settings > Default apps.
        Registering it even when the checkbox is cleared means a user who
        changes their mind later can still find us there.
      -->
      <Component Id="PdfCapabilities" Guid="*">
        <RegistryKey Root="HKCU" Key="Software\Lichang\PDF Editor\Capabilities">
          <RegistryValue Name="ApplicationName" Type="string" Value="PDF Editor" KeyPath="yes" />
          <RegistryValue Name="ApplicationDescription" Type="string" Value="檢視、註解與編輯 PDF 文件。" />
          <RegistryKey Key="FileAssociations">
            <RegistryValue Name=".pdf" Type="string" Value="asia.lichang.pdf-editor.pdf" />
          </RegistryKey>
        </RegistryKey>
        <!-- The index Settings reads. Value is a registry path relative to the same root. -->
        <RegistryValue Root="HKCU" Key="Software\RegisteredApplications" Name="PDF Editor" Type="string" Value="Software\Lichang\PDF Editor\Capabilities" />
      </Component>

      <!--
        Conditional: only when the user ticked the box on DefaultPdfDlg.
        UILevel=5 keeps unattended/silent installs from quietly grabbing .pdf.
      -->
      <Component Id="DefaultPdfAssociation" Guid="*">
        <Condition>SETDEFAULTPDF="1" AND UILevel=5</Condition>
        <RegistryKey Root="HKCU" Key="Software\Classes\.pdf">
          <RegistryValue Type="string" Value="asia.lichang.pdf-editor.pdf" KeyPath="yes" />
        </RegistryKey>
      </Component>
    </DirectoryRef>
  </Fragment>
</Wix>
